# Copy to .env and fill in before any non-local deployment:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# docker-compose reads this file automatically. With DEBUG=1 (the default for
# the local demo) the backend falls back to fixed development values and warns
# about it on every boot. With DEBUG=0 it REFUSES TO START unless both secrets
# below are set -- a missing secret must never quietly turn authentication off.
#
# Nothing here is needed for the default `docker-compose up --build`: every
# port is bound to 127.0.0.1 and the development values are in force. The LAN
# section at the bottom is the one exception worth reading before a live demo.

# Signs session tokens (HMAC-SHA256). Generate one with:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
DEEPCHECK_SECRET=

# Guards the SOC dashboard endpoints (GET /api/sessions, GET /api/score/{id}).
# The analyst types this into the dashboard's key prompt. It is deliberately
# NOT built into the frontend bundle: anything compiled into the bundle is
# served to every visitor, which would make the header check decoration.
DASHBOARD_KEY=

# 1 = local demo (development fallbacks allowed). 0 = production.
DEBUG=1

# Comma-separated browser origin allowlist for CORS. "*" is the default and is
# fine for a laptop demo only.
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://demo.example.com,https://soc.example.com

# Step-up verification code accepted by POST /api/demo/verify and shown in the
# demo's verification modal. A stand-in for a real SMS / 3-D Secure provider.
DEMO_VERIFY_CODE=482913

# Backend URL compiled into the frontend bundle at BUILD time. Change it and
# rebuild the frontend image; it is not read at container start. It is the
# address the visitor's BROWSER uses, so "localhost" means the visitor's own
# device.
# VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Host address the backend (8000) and frontend (3000) ports are published on.
# 127.0.0.1 (the default) keeps them off the network. Postgres stays on
# 127.0.0.1 regardless. Do not set 0.0.0.0 while DEBUG=1: the development
# secrets are public, so anyone on the network could forge session tokens and
# read the dashboard. See the LAN section below.
# BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# Postgres credentials. docker-compose builds the backend's DATABASE_URL from
# the same three values, so they only need setting here. The password goes
# into a URL: use letters, digits, "-" and "_" only (token_urlsafe output
# qualifies).
#
# Postgres applies these only when it creates an EMPTY data volume. Changing
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD on a machine that has already run the stack does not
# change the database's password; the backend then fails to authenticate.
# Either set it before the first `up`, or recreate the volume -- which DELETES
# every stored session:
#   docker-compose down -v && docker-compose up --build
# Host-side tools such as record_session.py connect through
# 127.0.0.1:5432 and need the same credentials, e.g.
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://deepcheck:<password>@localhost:5432/deepcheck
# POSTGRES_USER=deepcheck
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=deepcheck
# POSTGRES_DB=deepcheck

# Data retention. Raw telemetry (the recorded mouse path, click and key
# timestamps) is blanked after RAW_RETENTION_HOURS, leaving the six features
# and the score; whole rows go after ROW_RETENTION_HOURS. Recordings of real
# people should not accumulate indefinitely, and nothing after scoring needs
# them except record_session.py, which runs promptly.
# RAW_RETENTION_HOURS=1
# ROW_RETENTION_HOURS=24
#
# Per-customer profile retention, swept separately from the telemetry above
# (its own transaction and lock, so a failure in one never stops the other).
# An idle profile and its stored session vectors go after
# PROFILE_RETENTION_DAYS without a decision; an objection record is never
# swept, because it is what stops a refused profile from being recreated.
# Decision audit rows (the evidence for a human review of a contested
# decision) and profile access audit rows have their own clocks. Policy
# defaults to be confirmed in the DPIA, not measurements.
# PROFILE_RETENTION_DAYS=180
# DECISION_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# PROFILE_ACCESS_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# /api/demo/charge and /api/demo/verify. Defaults to DEBUG. Their step-up code
# is a fixed constant the demo page prints on screen, so any session the server
# answers "verify" for can be upgraded to "allow" by anyone who reads it. That
# is the point in a demo and unacceptable in a deployment.
# DEMO_ENDPOINTS=0

# Return the SHAP breakdown to the client being scored. Off by default: naming
# the features that convicted a caller lets it tune against the live detector.
# The dashboard still gets the full explanation from GET /api/score/{id}.
# SHAP_IN_ANALYZE=0

# Escalate sessions that share a behavioural signature with many others.
# Off by default on the evidence: against the adversarial harness it challenged
# 8-40% of legitimate sessions to catch 4-60% of mimics, depending on the
# threshold, and a control that flags more customers than attackers is worse
# than no control. The signature is still computed and stored, because it is
# where identity signals (device, TLS, IP) would attach -- those cluster far
# more reliably than behaviour does.
# CLUSTER_ESCALATION=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-customer behavioural profile (OFF unless every variable below is set)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A customer's own behavioural history may ask for EXTRA VERIFICATION and
# nothing else: it never blocks, never approves and never changes a risk score.
# It is the only control aimed at human account takeover and does nothing
# against card-testing bots. It has NOT been measured on real customers, so it
# ships off. The local demo needs none of this: with these unset, every
# endpoint behaves exactly as it does without the layer.
#
# There is no development fallback for ANY of these, DEBUG=1 included. The
# layer is on only when PROFILE_LAYER=1 AND DEEPCHECK_PROFILE_KEY is set AND at
# least one merchant is configured.
#
# Merchant credentials, "id:key" pairs, comma separated. A merchant backend
# sends X-Merchant-Id / X-Merchant-Key with every customer_ref and with the
# consent, erase and outcome calls. id: lowercase letters, digits, "_" or "-",
# at most 32 characters, starting with a letter or digit; "demo" is reserved.
# key: at least 32 printable ASCII characters, no spaces, different for every
# merchant and different from every other secret here. A malformed entry
# stops the backend from starting, in every mode.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# DEEPCHECK_MERCHANT_KEYS=acme:<token>,globex:<another token>
#
# The HMAC key that turns (merchant, customer reference) into the stored
# profile id; the raw reference is never stored. Must differ from
# DEEPCHECK_SECRET, DASHBOARD_KEY and every merchant key (checked at boot).
#
# ROTATING IT IS A SILENT MASS RESET. A pseudonym cannot be re-keyed without
# the raw references, which this system deliberately never keeps, so a new
# key re-derives every profile id: every customer becomes a stranger at once,
# the old profiles are orphaned until the idle-retention sweep removes them,
# no erasure or objection request can match them any more, and an objection
# recorded under the old key no longer refuses a consent under the new one
# (objection records are never swept, but they can no longer be found). Treat the key
# as long-lived, and its compromise as re-identifying the whole profile table.
# DEEPCHECK_PROFILE_KEY_VERSION is written on every profile, and a profile
# stamped with a different version is never compared: it exists so a future
# rotation can dual-write for a window instead. Bump it only together with a
# key change.
# DEEPCHECK_PROFILE_KEY=
# DEEPCHECK_PROFILE_KEY_VERSION=1
#
# PROFILE_LAYER=1 computes, stores and audits the layer's opinion (shadow
# mode). PROFILE_ESCALATION=1 additionally lets it ask for verification, and
# is ignored while PROFILE_LAYER is off. Do not turn escalation on before the
# layer has been measured on a real person across sessions and devices.
# PROFILE_LAYER=0
# PROFILE_ESCALATION=0
#
# Per-operator credentials for GET /api/profile/review/{session_id}, the human
# review of a contested decision (GDPR Art. 22(3), KVKK 11(1)(g)): the
# customer's stored session vectors and what the layer said about them.
# Same "id:key" format and rules as DEEPCHECK_MERCHANT_KEYS, one entry per
# PERSON, and every key different from every merchant key, the profile key
# and the dashboard key (checked at boot). Deliberately not DASHBOARD_KEY: that
# is one shared password, and every review is written to profile_access_audit
# under the operator id, which a shared key cannot attribute to anyone. Unset
# means nobody can review. Not tied to PROFILE_LAYER, so a decision made while
# the layer was on stays reviewable after it is switched off.
# PROFILE_REVIEW_KEYS=denetci-ayse:<token>

# Leading zero bits required of the session proof of work. 12 costs Chromium
# about 75 ms and a native script about 2 ms -- the asymmetry runs the wrong
# way, because JavaScript SHA-256 is roughly thirty times slower than native,
# so raising this taxes real customers harder than it taxes attackers. Treat it
# as evidence that code executed, not as a cost barrier.
# POW_DIFFICULTY_BITS=12

# Uvicorn worker processes (entrypoint.sh). Each holds its own model in memory.
# UVICORN_WORKERS=2

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAN demo: letting a juror open the demo on their own phone
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The defaults keep everything on 127.0.0.1, and the bundle points the browser
# at http://localhost:8000 -- which on a phone is the phone. All of the steps
# below are needed; any one missing gives a page that loads and then cannot
# score, verify or charge.
#
#  1. Find the laptop's LAN address: `ipconfig` (Windows), `ip -4 addr`
#     (Linux) or `ipconfig getifaddr en0` (macOS). 192.168.1.20 below.
#
#  2. Put these in .env (real values, not the placeholders):
#       DEBUG=0
#       DEEPCHECK_SECRET=<python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))">
#       DASHBOARD_KEY=<a second, different token>
#       DEMO_ENDPOINTS=1          # DEBUG=0 turns the demo checkout off otherwise
#       CORS_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.20:3000,http://localhost:3000
#       VITE_API_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8000
#       BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
#     DEBUG=0 is not optional. With DEBUG=1 the token secret and dashboard key
#     are the published development values, and BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0 puts them in
#     reach of everyone on the network.
#
#  3. Rebuild, because VITE_API_URL is compiled into the bundle:
#       docker-compose up --build
#
#  4. Allow inbound TCP 3000 and 8000 in the host firewall if the phone cannot
#     connect, then open http://192.168.1.20:3000/demo on the phone.
#
# What still holds and what does not, over plain http on a LAN:
#  - The SDK works. Its session proof of work hashes with crypto.subtle, which
#    browsers only expose on https or localhost; on http://<LAN-IP> it falls
#    back to a bundled JavaScript SHA-256 instead of failing to register.
#  - Nothing is encrypted in transit. Session tokens, and the dashboard key
#    when the SOC dashboard is opened from another device, cross the Wi-Fi in
#    clear text. Use keys generated for the demo and replace them afterwards.
#  - The demo verification code is printed on the page, so anyone on the
#    network can pass step-up. That is what DEMO_ENDPOINTS=1 means.
#
# Afterwards, remove BIND_ADDR (or set 127.0.0.1) and rebuild with the default
# VITE_API_URL.
